<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-a-nonuniform-rational-localization">
  <title>A nonuniform rational localization</title>
  <p>
    We next compute the rational chart which witnesses the failure of stable
uniformity.
  </p>
  <p>
    Consider the datum
<me>\alpha_{\mathrm{in}}=(W;\varpi).</me>
It is a genuine rational datum: the ideal <m>(\varpi,W)</m> is open (indeed <m>\varpi</m> is a unit in <m>A</m>).  Let
<me>A_{\mathrm{in}}=A\left\langle\frac{W}{\varpi}\right\rangle</me>
be the corresponding rational localization on <m>|W|\leq |\varpi|</m>, and write
<m>X=W/\varpi</m>.  By the ring-of-definition construction, <m>A_{\mathrm{in}}</m> is the separated completion of the subring generated by <m>A_0</m> and <m>X</m>.
  </p>
  <proposition xml:id="prop-bad-chart">
    <statement>
      <p>
        There is a canonical isomorphism of complete topological rings
<me>A_{\mathrm{in}}\cong k\langle X,Q\rangle/(Q^2),
  \qquad W\longmapsto\varpi X.</me>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <lean project="AdicSpaces" ref="FiniteJetOver.chartDatum_isRational">Lean (chartDatum_isRational)</lean>
    <lean project="AdicSpaces" ref="FiniteJetOver.chartEquiv">Lean (chartEquiv)</lean>
    <lean project="AdicSpaces" ref="FiniteJetOver.chartEquiv_continuous">Lean (chartEquiv_continuous)</lean>
    <lean project="AdicSpaces" ref="FiniteJetOver.chartEquiv_symm_continuous">Lean (chartEquiv_symm_continuous)</lean>
    <lean project="AdicSpaces" ref="FiniteJetOver.chartEquiv_canonicalMap_W">Lean (chartEquiv_canonicalMap_W)</lean>
    <proof>
      <p>
        Let
<me>q\colon A_0[X]\longrightarrow A[X]/(\varpi X-W)</me>
be the composite of the inclusion <m>A_0[X]\hookrightarrow A[X]</m> with the
quotient map, and set
<me>G_0:=q(A_0[X]).</me>
Since <m>\varpi</m> is a unit in <m>A</m>, evaluation at <m>X=W/\varpi</m> gives a
canonical isomorphism
<me>A[X]/(\varpi X-W)\xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }A.</me>
Under this identification,
<me>G_0=A_0[W/\varpi]\subset A;</me>
it is the ring of definition whose separated <m>\varpi</m>-adic completion
defines <m>A_{\mathrm{in}}</m>.  For <m>y\in Q^2C_0</m> and every <m>n\geq 0</m>, the
support condition gives <m>W^{-n}y\in A_0</m>.  In <m>G_0</m> one has
        <men xml:id="eq-infinitely-divisible">y=W^n(W^{-n}y)=\varpi^nX^n(W^{-n}y)\in\varpi^nG_0.</men>
        Thus <m>Q^2C_0</m> maps to zero in the separated completion.  After multiplying by a suitable power of <m>\varpi</m>, the same conclusion holds for every element of <m>Q^2C</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Put
<me>T_0=k^\circ\langle X,Q\rangle/(Q^2),
  \qquad T=T_0[1/\varpi].</me>
Using the unique expression in <xref ref="eq-support-A0"/>, define
<me>f_0(W)+Qf_1(W)+Q^2h
  \longmapsto
  f_0(\varpi X)+Qf_1(\varpi X).</me>
This sends <m>A_0</m> into <m>T_0</m>, and it sends <m>W/\varpi</m> to <m>X</m>.  Hence it extends to a continuous homomorphism
<m>A_{\mathrm{in}}\to T</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Conversely, <m>X</m> and <m>Q</m> belong to the ring of definition of <m>A_{\mathrm{in}}</m>, and
<xref ref="eq-infinitely-divisible"/> gives <m>Q^2=0</m> there.  They therefore define a continuous homomorphism <m>T\to A_{\mathrm{in}}</m>.  The two composites fix <m>X</m> and <m>Q</m>.  On the dense image of <m>G_0</m>, the second composite differs from the identity only by an element of <m>Q^2C_0</m>, which is zero by <xref ref="eq-infinitely-divisible"/>.  The two maps are therefore inverse.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  <proposition xml:id="prop-not-stably-uniform">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The ring <m>A</m> is not stably uniform.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <lean project="AdicSpaces" ref="FiniteJetOver.not_isUniform_chart">Lean (not_isUniform_chart)</lean>
    <lean project="AdicSpaces" ref="FiniteJetOver.finiteJet_not_stablyUniform_of_dvr">Lean (finiteJet_not_stablyUniform_of_dvr)</lean>
    <proof>
      <p>
        In <m>T=k\langle X,Q\rangle/(Q^2)</m>, every element of the line <m>kQ</m> is nilpotent and hence power-bounded.  This line is not bounded with respect to the ring of definition <m>T_0</m>: for every <m>r\geq 0</m>,
<me>\varpi^r\bigl(\varpi^{-(r+1)}Q\bigr)=\varpi^{-1}Q\notin T_0.</me>
Thus <m>T</m> is not uniform.  By <xref ref="prop-bad-chart"/>, a genuine rational localization of the uniform ring <m>A</m> is nonuniform.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
</section>
